FINDINGS = [
    ("Nine of the twenty already have their regulatory hook", "For nine opportunities the nuclear rule or programme that could carry the transfer exists today (SFCP and NEI 04-10, 10 CFR 63.114, MARSSIM, the approved reload methods, ANS-8.26, RG 1.203, ITAAC, Part 40 excursion limits, the financing gates). Those are graded direct: the work is engineering and adoption, not rule-making."),
    ("The gains cluster at 15 to 30 percent, and the outliers are owned data", "Turnaround overrun, mining unit cost, North Sea decommissioning, rigless abandonment, JCL cost growth, Brugge, Twin Creeks: the independently measured gains are 10 to 30 percent. The two-fold and larger results (Rolls-Royce time on wing, McLaren changeovers, NASA's 45-to-2 percent) came from an owner of the data and the risk, or from choreography, not from software alone. The map's 'exponential' verdicts survive only where the software changes what gets built or tested, not how fast."),
    ("Every credit was preceded by a validator, a floor or a verifier", "No regulator in the set credited a model directly. They credited a schema with a validator (P1), a process with a floor (P2), or a deterministic verifier around the optimiser (P3). The nuclear ask should be phrased in one of those three forms; asking the NRC to approve a model is the wrong question."),
    ("The regulator's own modellers are the rate-limiting resource", "FDA's Bayesian approvals were run by FDA statisticians; Florida's commission audits code on site; ECMWF ran its own parallel. The NRC's fastest-moving asset is its own use of language models on reviews; an office of computational review at NRC would do more for the twenty than any applicant tool."),
    ("Nuclear pays the whole bill and still lacks the benchmark", "NSTA's leverage came from the Treasury's share of decommissioning cost; DOE EM and the NDA pay all of it and have no per-facility public baseline. The single cheapest institutional change in the atlas is a published, annually re-estimated P50 per site."),
    ("The seeded-set protocol is one page and nobody has written it for nuclear", "Knapp-Kushner, precisionFDA and the FRA test programme all reduce to: build a set with planted defects, run the machine and the certified humans, publish both scores. TRISO inspection, waste sorting and automated NDE records each need exactly that document, and it is the shortest path to a first regulatory credit for a learned classifier in nuclear."),
]
